== Part E | Sellers and Costs

_Sellers decisions make up the supply curve._

market power breaks the efficiency of markets

Then we'll turn to the sellers' side of the market to understand how they choose quantity supplied. This will allow us to understand in more detail how market structure matters for welfare. To do this, we start with the simplest case of the firm's problem, which is price taking, constructing their individual supply curve, then the supply curves. This could be told from Molly's perspective.

This part aims to develop the model behind the supply curve.

Maybe start with a model of imperfect competition to introduce the MR curve. This might be a nice extension from the market with Molly, where there are many but not many many sellers.

=== Episode E1 | Costs of Production

Firms transform inputs into outputs through production functions and minimize costs to determine their supply decisions.

This video introduces costs as a way of measuring the tradeoffs firms face.

This video introduces production functions transforming inputs into outputs and how they give us isoquants. This might be better to not do for intro ...

=== Episode E2 | Competitive Firms

Perfect competition emerges when firms are price-takers with no individual market power over the equilibrium price.

This video builds the marginal revenue curve, doing that thing where demand moves from flat to downward sloping, showing that price is the demand curve in perfectly competitive markets.

=== Episode E3 | Monopoly

Market power allows firms to influence prices and capture profits at the expense of consumer welfare and economic efficiency.

=== Episode E4 | Duopoly

Strategic interaction between a few firms doesn't fully eliminate market power and it's resulting problems.

=== Episode E5 | Market Structures

Comparing perfect competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, and monopoly reveals how market structure determines economic outcomes.

